**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**

I am a lead maintainer of [LoopBack](https://loopback.io), a REST API framework for Node.js. Recently, [request](https://www.npmjs.com/package/request) has been deprecated (see https://github.com/request/request/issues/3142) and we started to look for alternatives (see https://github.com/request/request/issues/3143). We picked Axios as the best option.

However, while migrating from `request` to `axios`, we found small API incompatibilities that are making migration more tedious than I would like to.

- `request` options have `uri` field, `axios` has `url`
- `request` accepts `proxy` specified as a URL string (e.g. `http://localhost:8080`), `axios` requires the URL to be parsed into components
- `request` (and Node.js) provides `statusCode` property on the response object, `axios` provides `status` only
- `request` (and other popular clients like [superagent](https://github.com/visionmedia/superagent)) store the buffered response body in `body` property, `axios` uses `data` instead. (Also on the server, Express middleware provide `req.body` property.)

**Describe the solution you'd like**

- Enhance Axios to accept request-like configuration options as aliases to Axios options.
- Enhance the response object returned by Axios to provide property getters to offer request-like API (e.g. `class AxiosResponse { get body() { return this.data }}`).

**Describe alternatives you've considered**

Create a wrapper function that will massage request options from `request` to `axios` format and then add additional fields to the response object returned by axios.

```js
async function makeRequest(options) {
  if (!options.url && options.uri) {
    options.url = options.uri;
    delete options.uri;
  }
  // etc.
  const response = axios(options);
  response.body = response.data;
  response.statusCode = response.status;
  return response;
}
```

**Additional context**

I am happy to contribute a pull request to implement those enhancements. However, I'd like to first check with you which of them are likely to be accepted. I also see 147 open pull request, it makes me wonder how likely would such pull request be reviewed and merged?

